From 8d04eebe2bf18e3900072283232ff88ffd1894fe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Jonas=20=C3=85dahl?= <jadahl@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Mar 2026 14:45:39 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 13/20] Add out-of-process GTK4 plugin

This introduces a GTK4 based plugin that runs all of the GTK specific
bits in a dedicated process. The GTK4 client uses Wayland to connect to
the GTK4 plugin, which tunnels most of the protocol traffic directly to
the host compositor, with a few exceptions.

The are primarily three types of protocol traffic that is not directly
tunneled: xdg_wm_base, and a custom libdecor_shell protocol. While the
GTK4 client interfaces using xdg_wm_base, xdg_toplevel, etc, this part
of the protocol traffic gets transformed, making the xdg_toplevel of the
GTK4 client showing the decorations a subsurface that is placed below
the application's main surface. The custom libdecor_shell protocol is
used to communicate with the client details about the content it is
decorating. It uses this information to layout an empty widget that
matches the content, and then communicates back enough information
allowing the plugin side to correctly position the subsurface.

The majority of the protocol tunnel code is generated by the python
script 'scanner.py'. Disclaimer: The code that parses the Wayland XML
file turning it into a tree of python data classes was originally
generated by an LLM but was heavily edited after generation.

The python script aims to be compatible with python 3.9.

Currently the wl_compositor is manually tunneled, due to the more hands
on management of surfaces.
